## Problem: `getConfig` lacks retry protection on transient connection loss

### Environment

- gbrain v0.41.26.0 (commit 2aed39b3)
- Postgres engine behind a connection pooler (port 5433)

### Background

The `conversation_facts_backfill` cycle phase (added in v0.41.11.0, included in `ALL_PHASES` by default) calls `engine.getConfig()` to check `cycle.conversation_facts_backfill.enabled`. When the Postgres connection briefly drops — a transient event — `getConfig()` throws immediately with `"No database connection: connect() has not been called"` and crashes the entire dream cycle with exit code 1.

This is the same class of transient connection loss that batch operations (`addLinksBatch`, `addTimelineEntriesBatch`, `upsertChunks`) handle via the `withRetry`/`batchRetry` mechanism introduced in v0.41.19.0 (#1537). But `getConfig()` was not covered.

### Root cause

`PostgresEngine.getConfig()` at `src/core/postgres-engine.ts:4432-4436` uses a bare `this.sql` call with no retry wrapper:

```typescript
async getConfig(key: string): Promise<string | null> {
    const sql = this.sql;
    const rows = await sql`SELECT value FROM config WHERE key = ${key}`;
    return rows.length > 0 ? (rows[0].value as string) : null;
}
```

The `sql` getter (`postgres-engine.ts:122-125`) has a two-tier fallback:

```typescript
get sql(): ReturnType<typeof postgres> {
    if (this._sql) return this._sql;
    return db.getConnection();
}
```

When a pooler (PgBouncer/Supavisor) recycles a connection, `this._sql` is set to `null` during `disconnect()` (`postgres-engine.ts:196-214`). If the global singleton in `db.ts` is also `null` (which happens when its `disconnect()` was called as part of the same pooler-initiated teardown, or when no separate module-level connection was ever opened), `db.getConnection()` throws `GBrainError('No database connection', 'connect() has not been called', ...)`.

The `conversation_facts_backfill` phase passes this error through to the cycle runner with no try/catch, causing the entire `gbrain dream` process to exit with code 1.

### Impact

- `gbrain dream` exits with code 1 when `conversation_facts_backfill` runs after a transient connection drop
- Reproduced in deployment: all prior dream phases (extract, patterns, consolidate, etc.) succeeded against the same engine, then `conversation_facts_backfill` hit a dropped connection and crashed
- The non-zero exit propagates to cron alerts and operator confusion

### Existing defense

v0.41.19.0 (#1537) added `withRetry(BULK_RETRY_OPTS)` — 3 retries, decorrelated jitter, up to ~12s total — but ONLY to `addLinksBatch`, `addTimelineEntriesBatch`, and `upsertChunks`. The commit message states that retry becomes a data-primitive contract inherited by all callers, but `getConfig()` and other unguarded methods (`listConfigKeys`) were not covered — only batch writes were addressed. The CI lint guard (`check-no-double-retry.sh`) only prevents double-wrapping engine batch methods — it does not ensure all engine methods have retry.

### Suggested fix

Add `withRetry` protection to `getConfig()`:

```typescript
import { withRetry, BULK_RETRY_OPTS } from './retry';

async getConfig(key: string): Promise<string | null> {
    return withRetry(async () => {
        const sql = this.sql;
        const rows = await sql`SELECT value FROM config WHERE key = ${key}`;
        return rows.length > 0 ? (rows[0].value as string) : null;
    }, BULK_RETRY_OPTS);
}
```

`BULK_RETRY_OPTS` is used for consistency with the existing engine-level retry pattern. While `getConfig` is a single-row read (not a bulk write), the same retry parameters are appropriate: transient pooler disconnects affect read and write operations identically, and a 12s worst-case wait is negligible for a config read.

An alternative approach — wrapping the `sql` getter itself to auto-retry — would introduce double-retry risk: methods already wrapped in `withRetry` would retry at both the getter level and their own wrapper level. The engine-level fix is the safer choice.

Summary

On a brain using a user-provided-models embedding recipe (llama-server, litellm — recipes that ship models: [] because the model id is whatever the user launched the server with), gbrain query silently degrades to keyword-only: semantic / cross-lingual queries return "No results", while queries with rare literal tokens still hit via the keyword arm.
The embedder itself is fine. embedQuery() succeeds (returns a correct 1024-dim vector) and engine.searchVector() returns the right semantically-ranked rows. The problem is that hybridSearch never runs the vector arm — it emits vector_enabled: false and returns only keyword results. 

Root cause

hybridSearch (src/core/search/hybrid.ts, ~line 603) gates the vector arm on:
tsconst { isAvailable } = await import('../ai/gateway.ts');
const providerProbe = resolvedCol.embeddingModel || undefined;
if (!isAvailable('embedding', providerProbe)) {
  // → emits vector_enabled: false, returns keyword-only
}
isAvailable (src/core/ai/gateway.ts) contains this guard:
tsconst isUserProvided =
  touchpoint === 'embedding' &&
  (touchpointConfig as any).user_provided_models === true;
if (
  Array.isArray(touchpointConfig.models) &&
  touchpointConfig.models.length === 0 &&
  (recipe.id === 'litellm' || isUserProvided)
) return false;

For llama-server the recipe declares touchpoints.embedding = { models: [], user_provided_models: true, ... } by design. So models.length === 0 && isUserProvided is always true, and the guard returns false regardless of whether a concrete model was supplied. Even though the caller passed "llama-server:daniel-embed" (a real, working model), isAvailable('embedding', "llama-server:daniel-embed") returns false.
The guard's intent (per its comment) is "this recipe needs a user-provided model" — i.e. unavailable when no model was chosen. But it fires even when a model was chosen, because it only inspects the recipe's static models array, never the concrete model id that was passed in.
embedQuery works because it never consults isAvailable; only hybridSearch uses it as a gate — so embedding works everywhere except in hybrid search, which is the one place that matters for gbrain query.

Minimal repro

Configure a brain with a local llama-server embedder: embedding_model = "llama-server:<id>", embedding_dimensions = 1024, provider_base_urls: { "llama-server": "http://127.0.0.1:<port>/v1" } in ~/.gbrain/config.json, with the server reachable.
gbrain embed --all (vectors populate; searchVector works).
gbrain query "<a semantic query with no rare literal tokens>" → "No results."
In-process: isAvailable('embedding', 'llama-server:<id>') → false; embedQuery(...) → 1024-dim vector; engine.searchVector(vec) → correct rows; hybridSearch(...) → 0 rows; vector_enabled=false.

Proposed fix (one line)

The guard should only fire when no concrete model id was supplied. resolveRecipe(modelStr) already parses the model id; gate on it:
tsconst { recipe, parsed } = resolveRecipe(modelStr);   // also capture `parsed`
// ...
if (
  !parsed.modelId &&                                  // only when NO concrete model was given
  Array.isArray(touchpointConfig.models) &&
  touchpointConfig.models.length === 0 &&
  (recipe.id === 'litellm' || isUserProvided)
) return false;

The earlier if (!modelStr) return false; still handles the truly-empty case, so this only narrows the over-broad guard. With the fix, isAvailable('embedding', 'llama-server:<id>') → true, hybridSearch runs the vector arm (vector_enabled=true), and gbrain query returns semantically-ranked results.
Why it's low-risk

A user who configured an embedding model and populated vectors clearly intends to use them; returning "unavailable" is never the right answer in that case.

The change is additive (one extra && condition); the no-model-supplied path is unchanged.
Affects every local-embedding deployment (llama-server, litellm) — these are exactly the privacy/cost-conscious self-hosters most likely to rely on hybrid search.

